text:
What you will learn
By the end of the guide, you should be able to:

explain the relationship among artificial intelligence, machine learning, deep learning, and generative AI;
distinguish a compelling demonstration from an evaluated application;
frame a user need as a specific task with inputs, outputs, constraints, and success criteria;
decide when a rule-based method, existing model, retrieval workflow, or human process is appropriate;
identify the data, permissions, and domain knowledge a prototype requires;
design prompts and structured outputs for a narrowly defined task;
understand how retrieval and tools can connect a model to relevant information or actions;
implement a small prototype with clear boundaries and reproducible configuration;
evaluate outputs against representative cases and a meaningful baseline;
examine factual errors, failure modes, privacy, bias, and human oversight;
document what the prototype does, where it fails, and what further testing is needed; and
demonstrate and communicate the prototype without overstating its readiness.

distinguish an AI capability from a useful application;
describe a task in terms of its users, inputs, outputs, and intended action;
compare rules, search, predictive models, and generative approaches;
define a baseline, evaluation cases, and measurable acceptance criteria;
identify information boundaries, failure costs, and human review points; and
produce a one-page problem brief before building a prototype.

Build evaluation cases that expose visual errors
A representative visual test set should contain more than clear examples. For an article-review prototype, include:

a legible figure with an unambiguous caption;
a figure whose axes or legend change the apparent interpretation;
a table with a footnote or differing units;
a scanned page with text-recognition errors; and
an image too poor to support a confident answer.

Record the expected claim and source location before examining the model output. Mark an abstention or review request as correct when the evidence is insufficient. Evaluate whether the system identifies the right figure or page, reads it correctly, and respects any limitation in the caption or surrounding text.

Choose a response shape that fits the workflow
Free-form paragraphs can be pleasant to read but difficult to check automatically. A small, explicit output structure makes missing fields and source references visible.
For the article example, a conceptual response could be:
{
  "study_aim": {
    "text": "The stated aim goes here.",
    "source": "Introduction, passage 2",
    "status": "supported"
  },
  "study_design": {
    "text": "The design goes here.",
    "source": "Methods, passage 1",
    "status": "supported"
  },
  "main_finding": {
    "text": "The main finding goes here.",
    "source": "Results, passage 4",
    "status": "supported"
  },
  "stated_limitations": {
    "text": null,
    "source": null,
    "status": "unsupported"
  }
}
The example shows the shape of an output, not a finding from a real article. In an actual implementation, give every passage a stable identifier and define allowed statuses precisely. A null value indicates that evidence was not found in the supplied material; it does not prove the full article lacks that information.

Validate the structure independently
A program can check that the response parses and contains exactly the expected fields. It can check types, permitted status values, required references for supported claims, and length limits. If a response fails these checks, ask for a correction or route it for review before presenting it as a completed draft.
A small illustrative Python check might be:
REQUIRED_FIELDS = {
    "study_aim",
    "study_design",
    "main_finding",
    "stated_limitations",
}
ALLOWED_STATUS = {"supported", "unsupported"}


def validate_draft(draft):
    if not isinstance(draft, dict) or set(draft) != REQUIRED_FIELDS:
        raise ValueError("Unexpected article-summary fields")
    for name, item in draft.items():
        if not isinstance(item, dict) or set(item) != {"text", "source", "status"}:
            raise ValueError(f"Invalid field structure: {name}")
        if item["status"] not in ALLOWED_STATUS:
            raise ValueError(f"Invalid status: {name}")
        if item["status"] == "supported":
            if not isinstance(item["text"], str) or not item["text"].strip():
                raise ValueError(f"Missing supported text: {name}")
            if not isinstance(item["source"], str) or not item["source"].strip():
                raise ValueError(f"Missing source reference: {name}")
        elif item["text"] is not None or item["source"] is not None:
            raise ValueError(f"Unsupported field must be empty: {name}")
This is illustrative chapter code, not a complete executable program. It checks whether the response follows the contract. It cannot determine whether “Results, passage 4” exists or supports the claim. That second question requires comparing the output with the original article or an independently prepared answer.

Prepare material for retrieval
Document preparation determines what the search system can find. At minimum, retain:

a stable article identifier and approved source location;
page, section, or passage identifiers visible to a reviewer;
the extracted text and a way to inspect the original page;
boundaries between article body, captions, tables, and references; and
information about extraction failures or unreadable material.

A chunk is a piece of source text stored for retrieval. Chunk boundaries affect context. A tiny chunk may omit the qualification following a result; a huge chunk may contain several claims and obscure the relevant sentence. Keep enough surrounding text to interpret a claim and preserve a link back to its source. Test different boundaries only when observed errors justify the change.

Practice: design the information boundary
Draw or describe your own workflow from permitted source to reviewed output. For each step, state:

what input it receives;
what output it produces;
which error it could introduce;
what source identifier or trace it preserves; and
whether it can read, compute, write, or communicate externally.

Prepare three evaluation cases: one where keyword search finds the evidence, one where different wording challenges the search, and one where no supplied passage supports the requested answer. Add a test document containing an irrelevant instruction. Record the correct retrieval and final-answer behavior before running the system.

Pick a task, not a technology demonstration
Start with a real user and one repeatable unit of work. The continuing example is a reviewer who prepares a four-field summary of an approved research article. The prototype may draft the study aim, study design, main finding, and stated limitations, with passage references. A reviewer checks every field before using it.
The first version does not need to process an entire literature collection, interpret every figure, publish a report, or make a research decision automatically. It needs to answer one useful question: Does an evidence-linked draft help the reviewer complete this defined summary reliably and with acceptable effort?
A candidate task is suitable for the hackathon if:

a specific person can describe how they do it now;
permitted example inputs are available;
the expected output can be reviewed;
a simple baseline can be completed in the available time;
failure cases can be named in advance; and
the scope is small enough to build and evaluate honestly.

If the task depends on inaccessible documents, uncertain permission, or an unreviewable outcome, narrow it or choose another task.

Prepare an inspectable article
Assign a stable article ID, confirm permission to process it, and extract readable text. Preserve headings, page locations where available, and passage IDs. Inspect the beginning, a results passage, and the limitations or discussion section against the original. If extraction omits pages or corrupts key values, correct the input or mark the case for manual handling.
For a compact pilot, passages can be prepared in a simple structured file:
{
  "article_id": "article-017",
  "passages": [
    {
      "id": "Introduction-02",
      "section": "Introduction",
      "text": "[Verified excerpt from the approved article]"
    },
    {
      "id": "Methods-01",
      "section": "Methods",
      "text": "[Verified excerpt from the approved article]"
    }
  ]
}
The bracketed strings are placeholders, not real study content. Keep the original document available to the authorized reviewer. Do not commit confidential source text to a public repository.

Use the Chapter 07 output contract
The draft should contain study_aim, study_design, main_finding, and stated_limitations. Each field has text, source, and status. A supported field needs substantive text and a resolvable source; an unsupported field has empty text and source. Apply the structural validation described in Chapter 07 before sending anything to a reviewer as a completed draft.
A source-ID check can be expressed simply:
supplied_ids = {passage["id"] for passage in passages}

for field_name, item in draft.items():
    if item["status"] == "supported":
        if item["source"] not in supplied_ids:
            raise ValueError(f"Unknown source for {field_name}")
This illustrative check detects invented or mismatched identifiers. It does not show that the cited passage supports the wording. The reviewer must still read it in context. If a claim combines multiple passages, revise the output contract to accept a list of references and check every item.

Practice: complete one evaluated run
Using an approved development article, perform the full workflow and save a run record with:

source and permission status;
extraction and selection results;
prompt, model, and workflow versions;
provisional four-field draft;
structure and reference validation;
reviewer judgments and corrections;
total effort and any failures.

Then freeze the version and run the reserved cases. Compare them with the baseline and prepare a short error log. Do not delete unsuccessful runs; they show where the workflow needs work.

Plan one controlled iteration
If you continue, choose a change tied to the error log. Write down:

the observed failure and its frequency or severity;
the proposed change and the stage it affects;
the expected improvement and possible trade-off;
the development cases used while making the change; and
fresh or still-reserved cases for the next comparison.

For example, expanding passage context might reduce missed qualifications but increase irrelevant material and request cost. Test both effects. If a change alters the permitted data, external actions, or reviewer responsibility, revisit the Chapter 10 risk-and-oversight record before running it.

Practice: present the evidence
Prepare a five-minute demonstration and a one-page decision memo. The memo should contain:

the user, task, and scope;
what the prototype and baseline each did;
the size and composition of the reserved case set;
results for support, coverage, critical errors, and total reviewer effort;
one traced success and one traced failure;
data and human-review boundaries; and
a clear next decision with its evidence.

Invite a reviewer to ask: “Where did this claim come from?” and “What happens when the source does not answer?” If the demonstration cannot answer either question, improve the evidence path before expanding the prototype.

Set ownership and documentation
Assign responsibility for source permissions, technical operation, review criteria, evaluation, and changes to the model or prompts. Document:

the supported user task and eligible inputs;
the data flow and retention rules;
the output contract and human approval process;
the baseline and acceptance criteria;
known limitations and excluded cases;
the response to extraction, retrieval, or model failures; and
the process for changing and re-evaluating the workflow.

Keep a user-facing explanation concise: what the application can help draft, what sources it uses, and what a reviewer still checks. Keep technical records detailed enough for the responsible team to investigate errors and reproduce comparisons.
